---
export const metadata = {
  title: "Yesteryear",
  author: "Caro Claire Burke",
  year: 2025,
  rating: "4/5",
  completed: true,
  summary:
    "A reflection on nostalgia, idealized identities, tradwifery, and the search for belonging through Natalie and Caleb.",
};

const title = metadata.title;
const author = metadata.author;
const year = metadata.year;
const rating = metadata.rating;
const yearDisplay = year ?? "—";
const status = metadata.completed ? "Completed review" : "Half-baked review";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />

  <title>{title} Review</title>

  <style>
    :root {
      --bg: #f8f8f5;
      --text: #111;
      --muted: #666;
      --border: #d8d8d8;
      --max-width: 72ch;
    }

    html {
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
    }

    body {
      margin: 0;
      background: var(--bg);
      color: var(--text);
      font-family: Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 19px;
      line-height: 1.72;
      padding: 3rem 1.5rem 6rem;
    }

    .container {
      max-width: var(--max-width);
      margin: 0 auto;
      position: relative;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: 3rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
    }

    .meta {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-family: monospace;
      margin-bottom: 3rem;
    }

    .review-status {
      display: inline-block;
      color: var(--muted);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      padding: 0.2rem 0.55rem;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    p {
      margin-bottom: 1.7rem;
      text-align: justify;
    }

    .review-incomplete {
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--muted);
    }

    a {
      color: inherit;
    }

    hr {
      border: none;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      margin: 4rem 0;
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
      body {
        font-size: 17px;
        padding: 2rem 1.2rem 4rem;
      }

      h1 {
        font-size: 2.4rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
  <main class="container">

    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <div class="meta">
      {author} · {yearDisplay} · {rating}
    </div>

    <div class="review-status">
      {status}
    </div>

    <p class="review-incomplete">
      This is a half-baked review. Thoughts are unfinished, arguments
      remain underdeveloped, and further revisions are pending.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>Yesteryear</em> follows the life of Natalie Heller Mills, a
      tradwife who wakes up to the breakdown of her carefully crafted
      life. Her existence becomes so consumed by feigning that she
      eventually attempts to escape reality and become the very mask
      she wears.
    </p>

    <p>
      When reading the prologue, I initially wanted to put down the
      book and slap another “DNF” (did not finish) on my reading list.
      I didn't wish to read another book criticizing tradwifery. I had
      read more than enough op-eds on the subject and watched enough
      TikTok warriors decrying it as oppressive. I wanted to dismiss
      the book just as I had dismissed them: let a woman make her own
      choices, even if those choices include manually making butter
      or milking cows. And even if it's mostly fake, who cares? It
      inspires people to pursue their own bovine-related dreams.
      Upon reflection, however, I realized that my own mental
      reactions eerily paralleled the mental defenses the main
      character puts up.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before putting the book down, I read the dust jacket and
      decided to at least give it a chance, if only to discover the
      main character's reactions to being teleported back in time.
      I will admit that I discovered something about myself: I am
      exceedingly charitable toward alternative lifestyles. It's
      easy to construct an idealized past or alternative way of life
      that airbrushes away the problems of modernity. I could very
      well have been someone who, although not a loyal follower of
      the tradwife movement, would nevertheless support the decision
      if I heard of someone embracing it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The book is especially relevant given that nostalgia often
      stems from present uncertainty. It feels as though we are
      living through the most rapid period of development in history:
      new technologies emerging on a global scale, economies booming
      and busting within days. Everything familiar is unfamiliar;
      everything known is unprecedented. Who among us doesn't do the
      same with our childhood? A simpler time without struggle and
      worry—it's easy to apply this lens backward. Imagining away
      the struggle and pain into clean order is especially easy
      because the harsh mundanities of life are forgotten. Natalie
      is forced to use hours to make the butter she discarded in an
      instant. Natalie forgets the hours women spent burning their
      fingers in lye to launder clothes. <em>Yesteryear</em> forces
      the reader to confront his own idealizations.
    </p>

    <p>
      Natalie's desire to become a tradwife stems from her need to
      control and "masculate" her husband, as well as her longing
      for a simpler, purer past in which moral decay had not yet
      swept across America. Her husband is a particularly
      interesting character, although his evolution is not a
      central focus of the novel. Through the few glimpses we get
      of him, we see his own misfortune and victimization. He begins
      as an innocent, unassuming man with little ambition, but his
      wife identifies this as a condemnable character flaw. He is
      constantly pressured to become more "manly." The book
      identifies the false dichotomy imposed on men: they are either
      weak or manly and ruthless. Men are told to be strong, stoic,
      and focused, but forcing them all into this character type is
      a grave mistake. As always, the middle ground seems to be just
      right: strong but compassionate.
    </p>

    <p>
      Feeling so rejected by society, Caleb turns to the manosphere,
      where he finds a much-needed sense of belonging. The book does
      an admirable job of condemning traditional social expectations
      of masculinity, but I wonder whether it sufficiently explores
      what an alternative might look like.
    </p>

    <p>
      Caleb, for instance, eventually discovers what he wants to do:
      become a kindergarten teacher. Natalie dismisses this aspiration
      as insufficiently masculine and instead attempts to manufacture
      a more conventionally masculine identity for him through
      farming. Yet I find myself wondering whether the novel gives
      Caleb enough agency of his own. Although his desire to teach
      is perfectly legitimate, having a purpose and actively pursuing
      it are two different things. Caleb's predicament is therefore
      more complicated than simply being denied the freedom to pursue
      his ambitions: he has not learned to take responsibility for
      pursuing them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Caleb's lack of agency stems from his need for self-confidence,
      which he obtains through the manosphere. It offers him a
      twisted ideal of masculinity, but without it, he would need an
      alternative source of validation. A more nuanced interpretation
      suggests that perhaps Caleb does need certain masculine social
      norms but has not been exposed to positive examples of them.
      Perhaps not all traditional forms of masculinity are inherently
      harmful.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where do boys initially develop confidence? Through healthy
      interactions with other boys, participation in sports, clubs,
      and other social activities—which I can avouch—emphasize these
      norms. Perhaps traditional social norms aren't all bad; rather,
      the problem lies in their overemphasis and rigid enforcement.
      There is a difference between rejecting the expectation that
      men must be strong, stoic, and domineering and rejecting the
      value of strength, resilience, and camaraderie altogether.
      This distinction is one I wish the novel had explored more
      deeply, although it's quite understandable given the novel's
      focus on female identity.
    </p>

    <p>
      The book's main thesis, in my view, analyzes how we often
      romanticize idealized identities and lifestyles as a means
      of finding certainty and belonging, but in doing so, we risk
      overlooking the realities and contradictions underlying those
      ideals. Natalie's attempt at farm life ends up with her being
      a business owner and the dominant partner despite her ideal
      of a submissive wife.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <p class="review-incomplete">
      Half-baked review — to be continued.
    </p>

  </main>
</body>
</html>